# Debugging Standards

When investigating bugs, failures, or regressions:

# Universal Debugging Standards & Rules

These rules enforce evidence-based debugging, zero speculation, and deterministic regression isolation.

---

## 1. Reproduction Precedes Modification
- Never edit production logic without first reproducing the failure in a standalone script or test case.
- A reproduction must exit with a non-zero exit code (`1`) before any fix is attempted.

---

## 2. Red-Green Verification Proof
- A bug fix is only verified when the exact same reproduction script exits `0` without modification.
- Prove that the test did not pass spuriously (e.g. by commenting out the assertion).

---

## 3. Isolate via Git Bisect
- When diagnosing a regression in an existing codebase, do not guess which commit caused it.
- Use `git bisect` with an automated test command to locate the culprit commit authoritatively.

---

## 4. Deconstruct Stack Traces
- Trace errors from the bottom of the stack to the earliest user-space frame.
- Do not fix symptom lines in external library wrappers; trace back to the improper arguments passed from repository code.
